From 82a14d43efb8b0852a2a5a394ba348c7c327b8d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 21:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 8/8] netvsc: allocate RNDIS request descriptors with
 kvmalloc()

struct rndis_request embeds two RNDIS_EXT_LEN tails. RNDIS_EXT_LEN is
HV_HYP_PAGE_SIZE, so the two tails alone are 8 KiB and the struct is
just over KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE. get_rndis_request() therefore falls
through kzalloc() to kmalloc_large(), which asks for an order-2
compound page on every RNDIS control request.

That is a high-order allocation on the subchannel open path. The
fragmentation drill of this series reproduces it: with the buddy
holding a single order-2 block, the hv_netvsc rebind reaches

  kworker/0:1 invoked oom-killer:
    gfp_mask=0x40dc0(GFP_KERNEL|__GFP_ZERO|__GFP_COMP), order=2
  Workqueue: events netvsc_subchan_work
  ? ___kmalloc_large_node
  ? __kmalloc_large_noprof
  ? get_rndis_request
  ? rndis_set_rss_param_msg
  ? rndis_set_subchannel
  ? netvsc_subchan_work
  sh: page allocation failure: order:2, ...
  Kernel panic - not syncing: System is deadlocked on memory

Patch 7/8 removes the order-7 requestor array from that path. This
removes the order-2 request descriptor, which is the last high-order
allocation between the rebind and the ring. Without it the series does
not deliver the symptom it is written against.

kvmalloc() is the right allocator here rather than a smaller object.
The two RNDIS_EXT_LEN tails are protocol capacity -- a QUERY response
is copied into response_ext and checked against
sizeof(struct rndis_message) + RNDIS_EXT_LEN -- so shrinking them is a
behaviour change, not a cleanup. The request is a control-path object
copied into the VMBus ring; it is never DMA-mapped from this
allocation, so vmalloc backing is fine and is the same idiom patch 7/8
already uses for the requestor array. kvmalloc() attempts the kmalloc
path with __GFP_NORETRY|__GFP_NOWARN first, so it neither fails hard
nor reaches the OOM killer when no compound page is free -- which is
also what removes the oom-kill of the drill shell above.

Free side is put_rndis_request(), the single kfree() of the object,
and it becomes kvfree().

Rollback trigger: revert this patch if an RNDIS control path begins to
require the request descriptor to be direct-mapped or DMA-mapped, or
if a vmalloc-backed request introduces a measurable control-path
regression.

Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c b/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c
index c35f9685b6bf..82a14d43efb8 100644
--- a/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c
+++ b/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c
@@ -87,7 +87,20 @@ static struct rndis_request *get_rndis_request(struct rndis_device *dev,
 	struct rndis_set_request *set;
 	unsigned long flags;
 
-	request = kzalloc(sizeof(struct rndis_request), GFP_KERNEL);
+	/*
+	 * struct rndis_request embeds two RNDIS_EXT_LEN (4 KiB) tails and
+	 * is larger than KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE, so kzalloc() would hand
+	 * back an order-2 compound page. That is a high-order allocation
+	 * on the subchannel open path and it fails under buddy
+	 * fragmentation -- the same failure this series removes from the
+	 * rings and the requestor array. The request is a control-path
+	 * object copied into the VMBus ring and never DMA-mapped from
+	 * this allocation, so vmalloc backing is fine. kvmalloc() tries
+	 * the kmalloc path with __GFP_NORETRY|__GFP_NOWARN first, so it
+	 * neither fails hard nor reaches the OOM killer when no
+	 * compound page is available.
+	 */
+	request = kvmalloc(sizeof(*request), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!request)
 		return NULL;
 
@@ -124,7 +137,8 @@ static void put_rndis_request(struct rndis_device *dev,
 	list_del(&req->list_ent);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dev->request_lock, flags);
 
-	kfree(req);
+	/* Paired with the kvmalloc() in get_rndis_request(). */
+	kvfree(req);
 }
 
 static void dump_rndis_message(struct net_device *netdev,
